index 9123ac4..fb15f06 100644
--- a/docs/live-game.md
+++ b/docs/live-game.md
@@ -25,3 +25,7 @@ Conference Wi-Fi often drops WebSockets, so both screens poll over plain HTTP (a
 ## Storage
 
 Games, players, and answers live in the same SQLite database as quizzes (`storage/`). QR codes are drawn in-process with the `rqrcode` gem. No external services.
+
+## Prizes and speed
+
+See `docs/prizes.md`. Scoring is one point per correct answer, ties broken by the total time of correct answers. Opening a question schedules it 120ms ahead. Both screens poll about every 40ms and reveal on that shared moment, so the clock is fair and still feels immediate. After End quiz the big screen is a leaderboard and phones show place and points. "Top N", "Draw", and "Reset winners" pick prizes; 0 points never wins.
diff --git a/docs/prizes.md b/docs/prizes.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f106ffc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/prizes.md
@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+# Prizes
+
+A quiz has "Number of winners" (at least 1, default 3) on its settings page. Games read the current value when the moderator picks winners, so changing settings before the draw updates the "Top N" button.
+
+## Scoring
+
+- One point per correct answer. The last pick on a question is the one that counts.
+- Equal points are ordered by speed: the sum of response times of correct answers, lower first. A response time runs from the shared question-open moment to that last pick.
+- A remaining tie breaks by join order so Top N is stable. Players never see points until the quiz is over.
+
+## Same moment
+
+Speed is only fair if every screen starts the question together. Opening a question schedules it 120ms ahead (`Game::OPEN_LEAD_MS`). The big screen and the phones poll plain HTTP about every 40ms, then reveal on that shared timestamp. Test requests skip the lead so an answer posted immediately still counts. Conference Wi-Fi drops WebSockets, so this stays on plain HTTP. State polls are not written to the log.
+
+## After End quiz
+
+
+
+The big screen lists every player, in rank order, with place, name, and points ("1 point", "2 points", "0 points"). A phone shows "Place 2 of 5" and the points. "The quiz is over" stays on both.
+
+## Picking winners
+
+N is the quiz's number of winners. Players with 0 points never win. If fewer than N players scored, all of them win.
+
+- "Top N" takes the first N of the leaderboard (speed already broke point ties).
+- "Draw" gives a prize to everyone strictly above the N-th place's points, then samples the remaining prizes from everyone on exactly those points. Speed does not matter. Each click draws again.
+- "Reset winners" clears the marks.
+- Winners show "Winner" on the leaderboard and "You won!" on their phone. Both follow the next poll.
